<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hurricane Irma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2017">2017</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hurricane Irma was a Category 5 Atlantic hurricane that struck the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> in September 2017, one of the strongest recorded in the open Atlantic. It destroyed most buildings on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbuda">Barbuda</a>, forcing the evacuation of the whole island, and devastated Sint Maarten and other islands before hitting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hurricane-irma.jpg" alt="Hurricane Irma" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NOAA's GOES 16 Satellite · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Irma_2017-09-06_1230Z.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>A tropical wave left the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> on 27 August 2017 and developed into a tropical depression on 30 August, about 120 nautical miles southwest of the Cape Verde Islands. Six hours later it became Tropical Storm Irma, the ninth named storm of the 2017 Atlantic hurricane season. Over warm water and in low wind shear it intensified rapidly, becoming a hurricane on 31 August and a major hurricane by 1 September. After fluctuating between Categories 2 and 3, it became a Category 5 hurricane early on 5 September, and early on 6 September it peaked with sustained winds of 180 mph (290 km/h) and a minimum pressure of 914 hPa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Hurricane warnings were issued on 5 September for the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>. More than half of the residents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbuda">Barbuda</a> took shelter in expectation of a direct hit. The eyewall passed over Barbuda early on 6 September, and a weather station recorded a gust of 160 mph. Irma damaged or destroyed 95 per cent of the island's structures, including its hospital, schools and both hotels, and three people died there.</p><p>The hurricane also caused catastrophic damage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-barthelemy">Saint Barthélemy</a>, Saint Martin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anguilla">Anguilla</a> and the Virgin Islands, and damage to the harbour and airport of Sint Maarten left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> part of Saint Martin unreachable. On Ragged Island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a> officials declared the island uninhabitable.</p><p>Irma weakened briefly to Category 2 as it made landfall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, then regained Category 4 strength over the Straits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and struck Cudjoe Key on 10 September. It made a second landfall on Marco Island the same day, became a remnant low over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> and dissipated on 13 September over Missouri. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida-keys">Florida Keys</a> suffered the worst of the damage in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The hurricane caused at least 134 deaths, 92 of them in the contiguous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Damage to roads, harbours and airports in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> hampered the delivery of relief, and the British, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, French and United States governments sent warships and aircraft with supplies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, itself heavily damaged, sent 750 health workers to other affected countries. Irma was the first Category 5 hurricane on record to strike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-maria">Hurricane Maria</a> followed at the same intensity two weeks later. Because of the damage and loss of life, the World Meteorological Organization retired the name Irma in April 2018, and Idalia replaced it for the 2023 season.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hurricane Irma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hurricane Irma</b> forced the evacuation of Barbuda in 2017, and disputes over communal land on Barbuda have continued.</blockquote><a href="/state/antigua-and-barbuda">Antigua and Barbuda</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hurricane Irma</b> devastated the island in 2017, and Dutch reconstruction aid came with conditions on governance.</blockquote><a href="/state/sint-maarten">Sint Maarten</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hurricanes Irma</b> and Maria struck in 2017, and the territory continues to debate its status and its Danish and African heritage.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-virgin-islands">United States Virgin Islands</a></li></ul></section>
